ting himself to doing what the  nfl wants which is to put on a suit of  armor  on a uh on a a more slight body than the  nfl would want at the quarterback  position  we had russell wilson and kyler murray  roughly two hours apart on set at the  super bowl and there was a noticeable  difference between the two i wish i  would have had them back to back so i  bet just in my own sitting you know five  feet away from them or less russell  wilson has that suit of armor kyler  murray doesn't and my concern was that  his frame may not be conducive to  putting on that suit of armor that it  may limit him that it may make his  movements tighter and more more  clumsy than otherwise they would be it  just didn't look like you could find a  spot to put 15 pounds he just looks  small not just short but small and uh  you know they'll find out on on  whatever day it is that they do the  weigh-in because it's down to your  boxers it's down to your briefs it's you  know stand there for everyone to gawk at  and get on a scale and they'll know  exactly how tall he is and they'll know  exactly how much he weighs when it's  time to go for that weigh-in peter  and you know the other thing i think  about all of this mike that we have to  keep uh that we have to understand  kyler murray is obviously it's good  right now that he weighs 206. he is not  an nfl finished product right now  under any circumstances and so i think  everybody who looks at him right now  is going to say essentially oh okay  what's he look like now what's it  because this is all you can go on but i  think  let's say he would go to a team and sit  for a year or so and i have i have sort  of said hey look new england's got  six picks between 32 and 101.  if kyler murray comes down in the draft  and is there in the teens and the  patriots like him they're certainly  going to have the ammunition to go get  him well to me  that's a pretty good spot for kyler  murray to go or to a team with a veteran  quarterback where he 